course: dictionary
lesson: 2861
title: One word, many domains
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words change meaning depending on where you meet them. Domain is one of these. In history, a domain is land that a king rules. On a computer, it is the address of a website. The words around it tell you which meaning to use.
explain: Look at the context. «Maths is her domain» means maths is the area she knows best. «The kitchen was Gran's domain» means Gran was the one in charge there. In both sentences, a domain is an area that belongs to someone or that they control.
explain: On a screen, a domain is the name of a website, like example.com. Before you click a link, check the domain. The last part of the address shows you whose website it is, so you can decide whether to trust it.
fact: A domain is an area that someone owns, rules or knows well.
fact: «Maths is her domain» means maths is the subject she knows best.
fact: A domain name is the address of a website, like example.com.
fact: «Public domain» describes work that is free for anyone to use, such as Tennyson's poem «The Eagle».
fact: In maths, the domain of a function is the set of numbers you are allowed to put into it.
exercise: «The king ruled every part of his domain.» What is a domain here? | A maths sum with no answer | A small pet that lives in a castle | A kind of computer game | * An area of land that a king or lord owns or rules
exercise: Which of these is a domain name? | Glowing | Heavy | * example.com | Tuesday
exercise: «Maths is her domain.» What does this tell you? | Maths is a place she visits on holiday | Maths is a kind of website | She dislikes maths and avoids it | * She knows and works in maths better than anything else
test: «The kitchen was Gran's domain.» What does domain mean here? | * The place Gran was in charge of | A cold room with no windows | A computer address | A king's castle
test: In maths, what is the domain of a function? | The name of the function | The answer the function gives | * The set of numbers you are allowed to put into it | A type of graph
exam: Why should you check a website's domain before you click? | To make the page load faster | To count how many words are on the page | * To see whose website it is and whether it looks safe | To change the colours of the screen
